1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:10,000
To some it was a miracle. Others called it the triumph of illiteracy.

2
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:15,000
It revolutionized the way people learned about their world.

3
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:17,437
It's right and it's right. It's right to be terrible.

4
00:00:17,438 --> 00:00:19,000
Oh my, get out of the way, please.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:23,000
It changed forever the way people would be entertained.

6
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
I gotta straighten out that closet one of these days.

7
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:31,500
Now, radio out of thin air on Modern Marvel.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:50,060
Yesterday, December 7th, 1941,

9
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:56,420
a date which will live in infamy.

10
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:01,980
On December 8, 1941, 60 million Americans tuned in their radios

11
00:01:01,981 --> 00:01:04,640
to hear President Franklin Delano Roosevelt

12
00:01:04,641 --> 00:01:07,720
ask Congress to declare war against Japan.

13
00:01:07,721 --> 00:01:09,820
The Empire of Japan.

14
00:01:09,821 --> 00:01:13,780
On that day in 1941, radio occupied a position

15
00:01:13,781 --> 00:01:16,620
at the very center of American life.

16
00:01:16,621 --> 00:01:19,860
People relied on it for information, entertainment,

17
00:01:19,861 --> 00:01:24,860
very often religion, and as a lifeline to a world around them.

18
00:01:24,861 --> 00:01:28,260
No one knew the power of radio better than Roosevelt, who

19
00:01:28,261 --> 00:01:30,100
had used the medium so effectively

20
00:01:30,101 --> 00:01:34,800
to reassure Americans during the dark days of the Depression.

21
00:01:34,801 --> 00:01:37,220
Now once again, they listened to the familiar voice

22
00:01:37,221 --> 00:01:40,500
of their radio president in living rooms, offices,

23
00:01:40,501 --> 00:01:44,040
automobiles, restaurants, grocery markets, or gas

24
00:01:44,041 --> 00:01:44,780
stations.

25
00:01:44,781 --> 00:01:46,180
Radio was everywhere.

26
00:01:46,181 --> 00:01:47,800
It was taken for granted.

27
00:01:47,801 --> 00:01:51,500
Yet just a few short decades before, it didn't exist.

28
00:01:54,860 --> 00:02:05,100
Most people credit Guillermo Marconi with inventing the radio,

29
00:02:05,101 --> 00:02:09,100
and he is recognized as the inventor of the wireless telegraph,

30
00:02:09,101 --> 00:02:12,520
filing for his patent in 1896.

31
00:02:12,521 --> 00:02:14,820
But brilliant scientist though he was,

32
00:02:14,821 --> 00:02:18,820
Marconi had many collaborators in that he was the beneficiary

33
00:02:18,821 --> 00:02:21,160
and exponent of centuries of research

34
00:02:21,161 --> 00:02:23,820
in the field of magnetic electricity.

35
00:02:23,821 --> 00:02:26,720
In a sense, Marconi may have discovered radio,

36
00:02:26,820 --> 00:02:29,960
but previous discoveries by a host of scientists

37
00:02:30,060 --> 00:02:33,560
and great thinkers had given him a good idea where to look.

38
00:02:43,680 --> 00:02:46,440
Man's desire to wage war created the need

39
00:02:46,540 --> 00:02:48,820
to communicate quickly over distance

40
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:52,780
and thereby gain advantage over the enemy.

41
00:02:52,781 --> 00:02:55,680
Medieval knights reflected sunlight on their shields

42
00:02:55,780 --> 00:02:56,880
as signals.

43
00:02:56,980 --> 00:03:00,380
Napoleon soldiers rallied to trumpet and drum.

44
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:03,920
Native American Indians exchanged smoke signals,

45
00:03:04,020 --> 00:03:09,300
while American colonists communicated with flags.

46
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:11,300
In the early part of the 19th century,

47
00:03:11,400 --> 00:03:13,940
the discovery and development of the electromagnet

48
00:03:14,040 --> 00:03:17,540
made telegraphy communication over wire scientifically

49
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:19,900
possible.

50
00:03:19,901 --> 00:03:24,400
In 1835, Samuel Morse sent his first message in dots

51
00:03:24,500 --> 00:03:28,540
and dashes over telegraph wire from Washington to Baltimore.

52
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:30,440
What hath God wrought?

53
00:03:30,540 --> 00:03:31,940
By the beginning of the Civil War,

54
00:03:32,040 --> 00:03:35,020
the same country that was about to divide itself in conflict

55
00:03:35,120 --> 00:03:38,660
north against south had been stitched together east to west

56
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:42,360
with the completion of a transcontinental telegraph wire.

57
00:03:42,460 --> 00:03:44,400
Two years after the close of the war,

58
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:48,400
a transatlantic cable was laid.

59
00:03:48,401 --> 00:03:50,640
Not long after the telegraph lines went up,

60
00:03:50,740 --> 00:03:53,700
scientists became aware that electrical current was leaking

61
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:56,880
from the wires and magnetizing metallic objects

62
00:03:56,980 --> 00:03:58,880
at a considerable distance.

63
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:01,880
The reason for this remained a mystery until 1856,

64
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:04,980
when James Clark Maxwell, an English physicist,

65
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:08,620
offered mathematical proof that electrical impulses

66
00:04:08,720 --> 00:04:11,520
travel through space in the form of waves.

67
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:13,920
These electromagnetic waves, he wrote,

68
00:04:14,020 --> 00:04:15,920
were similar in form and equal in speed

69
00:04:15,921 --> 00:04:18,760
equal in speed to the velocity of light.

70
00:04:18,761 --> 00:04:22,560
Well, I think the history of radio starts with a man named

71
00:04:22,561 --> 00:04:27,120
Clark Maxwell in the 19th century, who really stands as one

72
00:04:27,121 --> 00:04:29,860
of the most important physicists of all time,

73
00:04:29,861 --> 00:04:32,520
and really as important as Newton.

74
00:04:32,521 --> 00:04:36,060
Clark Maxwell postulated the existence

75
00:04:36,061 --> 00:04:40,720
of electromagnetic waves, which of course are responsible

76
00:04:40,721 --> 00:04:44,320
not only for sound, but for all of the light

77
00:04:44,321 --> 00:04:45,880
that we have around us.

78
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:50,120
In 1887, Heinrich Hertz, a young German scientist,

79
00:04:50,220 --> 00:04:52,720
attempted to apply Maxwell's theory.

80
00:04:52,820 --> 00:04:56,260
He fashioned an apparatus that had two metal balls separated

81
00:04:56,360 --> 00:04:58,060
by a narrow gap.

82
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:02,160
He connected this device to a coil of wire, a battery, a switch,

83
00:05:02,260 --> 00:05:05,880
and a Leiden jar, a device for storing electricity.

84
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:07,380
With the flick of a switch, Hertz

85
00:05:07,480 --> 00:05:10,120
caused electrons to jump the gap between the balls

86
00:05:10,220 --> 00:05:12,580
by alternating the polarity of the balls

87
00:05:12,680 --> 00:05:14,920
from negative to positive.

88
00:05:14,921 --> 00:05:16,920
Hertz called this device an oscillator

89
00:05:17,020 --> 00:05:19,760
because the spark oscillated or switched back and forth

90
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:22,560
continuously as long as the alternating current was

91
00:05:22,660 --> 00:05:23,960
maintained.

92
00:05:24,060 --> 00:05:26,600
Experimenting further, Hertz attached a metal ball

93
00:05:26,700 --> 00:05:28,940
to each end of a length of wire.

94
00:05:29,040 --> 00:05:30,540
He fashioned the wire into a loop,

95
00:05:30,640 --> 00:05:33,200
but left a narrow gap at the ends.

96
00:05:33,300 --> 00:05:35,240
He then placed the wire loop near,

97
00:05:35,340 --> 00:05:37,340
but not touching, the oscillator.

98
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:39,380
When he turned the oscillator circuit on,

99
00:05:39,480 --> 00:05:42,340
a spark jumped between the gap and the loop.

100
00:05:42,440 --> 00:05:43,980
How could this happen, Hertz wondered,

101
00:05:43,981 --> 00:05:47,320
when the loop and the oscillator were not even connected.

102
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:49,380
The answer, Hertz speculated, lay

103
00:05:49,480 --> 00:05:52,720
in the existence of electromagnetic waves.

104
00:05:52,820 --> 00:05:54,660
These waves had traveled through the air

105
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:56,820
from the oscillator to the spark.

106
00:05:56,920 --> 00:06:00,660
This simple experiment was the first radio transmission.

107
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:03,040
Hertz's oscillator had been the transmitter,

108
00:06:03,140 --> 00:06:05,040
and his wire loop had been the receiver.

109
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:10,780
Five years later, 18-year-old Guillermo Marconi

110
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:13,600
read of Hertz's accomplishments and saw instantly

111
00:06:13,601 --> 00:06:17,140
the commercial possibilities of wireless communication,

112
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:19,340
especially in the shipping industry.

113
00:06:19,440 --> 00:06:21,700
He turned the third floor of his family home

114
00:06:21,800 --> 00:06:24,840
into a workshop, read everything he could get his hands on

115
00:06:24,940 --> 00:06:28,240
relative to Hertzian waves, and sought to take up where Hertz

116
00:06:28,340 --> 00:06:30,260
and others had left off.

117
00:06:30,360 --> 00:06:33,760
His parents had a very large estate in Italy.

118
00:06:33,860 --> 00:06:38,000
His mother was a Jameson from the Irish whiskey company.

119
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:42,260
And when Marconi could not get, the Italian government

120
00:06:42,261 --> 00:06:47,260
interested in his wireless telegraphy,

121
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:51,000
his mother took him to London and connected him

122
00:06:51,100 --> 00:06:53,140
with the British postal authorities.

123
00:06:53,240 --> 00:06:57,800
And they saw the potential of what wireless telegraphy

124
00:06:57,900 --> 00:07:01,580
would do and immediately snapped up the patent.

125
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:03,640
Though untrained as a scientist,

126
00:07:03,740 --> 00:07:05,720
Marconi was able, through trial and error,

127
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:09,220
to increase the efficiency of Hertz's oscillator.

128
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:11,560
Then he added a grounded antenna, which he found

129
00:07:11,561 --> 00:07:14,660
strengthened the waves, although he didn't know why.

130
00:07:14,760 --> 00:07:17,460
Later, he determined that by raising his antenna,

131
00:07:17,560 --> 00:07:20,600
he could increase his transmitting range.

132
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:23,000
By the time Marconi was settled in England,

133
00:07:23,100 --> 00:07:26,000
he had transmitted Morse code without wires,

134
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:28,140
an astounding nine miles.

135
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:31,540
In 1898, he transmitted the results of a yacht race

136
00:07:31,640 --> 00:07:33,880
from a tugboat, allowing newspapers

137
00:07:33,980 --> 00:07:36,680
to print stories of the race before the yachts

138
00:07:36,780 --> 00:07:39,320
had returned to shore.

139
00:07:39,321 --> 00:07:41,720
Ship owners began to purchase wireless systems

140
00:07:41,820 --> 00:07:44,660
and install them on their vessels.

141
00:07:44,760 --> 00:07:49,260
On March 27, 1899, Marconi sent a wireless signal

142
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:53,160
across the English Channel to France.

143
00:07:53,260 --> 00:07:55,640
Overshadowing some of Marconi's success,

144
00:07:55,740 --> 00:07:58,200
at least in his own mind, was the fact

145
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:00,340
that he thought in order to be a success,

146
00:08:00,440 --> 00:08:02,480
wireless had to be private.

147
00:08:02,580 --> 00:08:05,320
The telegraph and the fledgling telephone were private,

148
00:08:05,420 --> 00:08:08,180
connecting only a single caller and receiver.

149
00:08:08,181 --> 00:08:11,720
But wireless could be picked up by anyone with a receiver.

150
00:08:11,820 --> 00:08:16,180
Privacy, he felt, was the key to success in communications.

151
00:08:16,280 --> 00:08:18,220
But before signals could be isolated,

152
00:08:18,320 --> 00:08:22,420
they would have to be distinguished one from the other.

153
00:08:22,520 --> 00:08:25,560
In England, Sir Oliver Lodge had patented a plan

154
00:08:25,660 --> 00:08:28,160
for regulating the wavelength of the transmitter

155
00:08:28,260 --> 00:08:33,040
and tuning the receiver to pick up only that wavelength.

156
00:08:33,140 --> 00:08:35,580
Marconi bought the patent from him.

157
00:08:35,581 --> 00:08:38,080
As the new century dawned, the air was filled

158
00:08:38,180 --> 00:08:40,280
with wireless signals.

159
00:08:40,380 --> 00:08:43,580
But these consisted only of dots and dashes.

160
00:08:43,680 --> 00:08:46,820
In 1901, the first transatlantic messages

161
00:08:46,920 --> 00:08:48,760
were exchanged by wireless.

162
00:08:48,860 --> 00:08:51,100
Four years later, the SS Arapaho

163
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:53,740
used for the first time the international distress call

164
00:08:53,840 --> 00:08:55,540
SOS.

165
00:08:55,640 --> 00:08:59,000
Soon afterward, the entire US Navy was equipped

166
00:08:59,100 --> 00:09:00,400
with Marconi's equipment.

167
00:09:05,580 --> 00:09:12,920
On Christmas Eve in 1906, professional and amateur

168
00:09:13,020 --> 00:09:16,700
wireless operators in the area of Grant Rock, Massachusetts,

169
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:20,000
picked up a transmission they would never forget.

170
00:09:20,100 --> 00:09:22,160
Instead of the usual dots and dashes,

171
00:09:22,260 --> 00:09:25,600
they heard human voices and the strains of a violin

172
00:09:25,700 --> 00:09:27,200
playing an old Christmas hymn.

173
00:09:27,300 --> 00:09:30,500
The center of this transmission was a scientist named Reginald

174
00:09:30,600 --> 00:09:31,900
Aubrey Fessenden.

175
00:09:31,901 --> 00:09:36,900
When Fessenden first played his violin on Christmas Eve

176
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:41,940
and the signal operators first heard this music coming out

177
00:09:42,040 --> 00:09:44,640
on their earphones, can you imagine?

178
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:48,020
They thought there was some kind of magic was happening.

179
00:09:48,120 --> 00:09:52,920
What Fessenden did was he imprinted a voice

180
00:09:53,020 --> 00:09:54,420
on a radio wave.

181
00:09:54,520 --> 00:09:57,860
And he did this by creating, with the help

182
00:09:57,960 --> 00:10:00,020
of General Electric Company and a man

183
00:10:00,021 --> 00:10:02,460
and a man named Ernst Alexanderson,

184
00:10:02,560 --> 00:10:05,920
something called the Alexanderson alternator,

185
00:10:06,020 --> 00:10:09,320
which was essentially an electric generator

186
00:10:09,420 --> 00:10:13,760
that moves so fast that it created not just electricity,

187
00:10:13,860 --> 00:10:16,660
but electromagnetic waves.

188
00:10:16,780 --> 00:10:19,180
Pheasanton used a carbon microphone

189
00:10:19,280 --> 00:10:22,480
to convert his voice into an audio signal.

190
00:10:22,580 --> 00:10:25,820
His transmission technique was called amplitude modulation,

191
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:28,280
or AM, because it modulated or controlled

192
00:10:28,281 --> 00:10:31,020
the amplitude or height of the wave.

193
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:33,160
Pheasanton's Christmas Eve transmission

194
00:10:33,260 --> 00:10:35,680
introduced a new concept to the world,

195
00:10:35,780 --> 00:10:38,960
a transmission intended for anyone who could hear it,

196
00:10:39,060 --> 00:10:40,500
a broadcast.

197
00:10:40,600 --> 00:10:43,660
And since this new concept suggested an image of waves

198
00:10:43,760 --> 00:10:47,340
radiating outward in all directions from a central point,

199
00:10:47,440 --> 00:10:49,060
the term radio was born.

200
00:10:52,000 --> 00:10:54,180
The grand collaboration had resulted

201
00:10:54,280 --> 00:10:56,300
in history's most stunning achievement

202
00:10:56,301 --> 00:10:58,460
in the field of communications.

203
00:10:58,560 --> 00:11:01,340
But here is where the cooperation ended.

204
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:04,840
From this point, radio would no longer be a curiosity,

205
00:11:04,940 --> 00:11:06,340
a tinker as fancy.

206
00:11:06,440 --> 00:11:09,640
Overnight, it would become big business with fortunes

207
00:11:09,740 --> 00:11:11,840
to be made or grabbed.

208
00:11:11,940 --> 00:11:14,520
The coming decade would see bitter rivalries emerge

209
00:11:14,620 --> 00:11:17,280
in the struggle to control this new medium.

210
00:11:17,380 --> 00:11:20,500
It would see the rise of the powerful media corporation

211
00:11:20,600 --> 00:11:24,600
and the demise of the individual inventor.

212
00:11:24,601 --> 00:11:28,440
There are nearly twice as many radios in the U.S. as there are people.

213
00:11:29,040 --> 00:11:32,500
Radio Out of Thin Air on Modern Marvels will continue in a moment.

214
00:11:36,300 --> 00:11:39,780
Radio Out of Thin Air now continues on Modern Marvels.

215
00:11:42,540 --> 00:11:47,060
Pheasanton's broadcast had sent radio technology spinning off in a new direction,

216
00:11:47,860 --> 00:11:54,580
and soon inventors and wireless operators alike were experimenting with ways to more effectively broadcast the human voice.

217
00:11:54,600 --> 00:11:58,600
But a better, smaller generator and a more sophisticated receiver

218
00:11:58,700 --> 00:12:00,100
were needed.

219
00:12:00,200 --> 00:12:05,240
Early receivers contained mineral rocks called crystals.

220
00:12:05,340 --> 00:12:08,680
These changed the alternating current of the incoming wave

221
00:12:08,780 --> 00:12:11,820
into direct current, allowing the sound to be heard.

222
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:13,260
Crystal sets, as they were called,

223
00:12:13,360 --> 00:12:16,020
were inexpensive and popular.

224
00:12:16,120 --> 00:12:19,700
But since they operated solely off the lower power generated

225
00:12:19,800 --> 00:12:22,120
by the incoming signal, the operator

226
00:12:22,121 --> 00:12:25,320
had to wear headphones to hear anything.

227
00:12:25,420 --> 00:12:28,920
One of Marconi's technical advisors, John Ambrose Fleming,

228
00:12:29,020 --> 00:12:31,360
realized that building bigger generators

229
00:12:31,460 --> 00:12:35,860
was not the key to improve radio transmitters and receivers.

230
00:12:35,960 --> 00:12:38,840
Inspired by Edison's light bulb and the work of English

231
00:12:38,940 --> 00:12:41,840
scientist JJ Thompson involving electrons,

232
00:12:41,940 --> 00:12:45,480
Fleming built a glass valve or diode, which was the forerunner

233
00:12:45,580 --> 00:12:47,620
of the vacuum tube.

234
00:12:47,720 --> 00:12:50,040
This diode used the power of the electron

235
00:12:50,041 --> 00:12:54,440
rather than the mechanical device to capture radio waves.

236
00:12:54,540 --> 00:12:58,280
In 1906, inventor Lee DeForest modified Fleming's diode

237
00:12:58,380 --> 00:13:02,020
to create a new device which amplified the audio signal,

238
00:13:02,120 --> 00:13:04,460
making it easier to hear.

239
00:13:04,560 --> 00:13:07,020
It was the first electronic amplifier.

240
00:13:07,120 --> 00:13:09,220
He called it the Audion tube.

241
00:13:09,320 --> 00:13:12,800
And he made the invention that changed the world,

242
00:13:12,900 --> 00:13:16,940
and that was the radio tube as we know it today.

243
00:13:17,040 --> 00:13:19,000
But the curious thing is, Lee DeForest

244
00:13:19,001 --> 00:13:22,300
didn't understand what he had invented.

245
00:13:22,400 --> 00:13:26,000
In 1911, a 21-year-old electrical engineer named

246
00:13:26,100 --> 00:13:32,320
Edwin Howard Armstrong began to study de Forrest's Audion tube.

247
00:13:32,420 --> 00:13:35,680
He determined that electrons within moved back and forth

248
00:13:35,780 --> 00:13:38,620
or oscillated in a steady rhythm.

249
00:13:38,720 --> 00:13:41,120
This current could be fed back into the system

250
00:13:41,220 --> 00:13:44,700
to be amplified again, thereby greatly increasing

251
00:13:44,800 --> 00:13:46,620
the power of the signal.

252
00:13:46,621 --> 00:13:50,020
This principle became known as feedback or regeneration

253
00:13:50,120 --> 00:13:55,100
and remains a basic principle of radio today.

254
00:13:55,200 --> 00:13:59,100
With Armstrong's innovation, large, expensive generators

255
00:13:59,200 --> 00:14:02,400
could be replaced by small vacuum tubes.

256
00:14:02,500 --> 00:14:04,400
Radio became more practical.

257
00:14:04,401 --> 00:14:05,900
practical.

258
00:14:13,840 --> 00:14:19,380
On the night of April 14, 1912, the luxury steamship Titanic

259
00:14:19,480 --> 00:14:21,960
collided with an iceberg in the North Atlantic

260
00:14:22,060 --> 00:14:24,420
and began to sink.

261
00:14:24,520 --> 00:14:26,960
The operator of the ship's Marconi wireless system

262
00:14:27,060 --> 00:14:29,860
tapped out a desperate call for help.

263
00:14:29,960 --> 00:14:32,100
The liner Carpathia picked up the signal

264
00:14:32,101 --> 00:14:36,000
came to the rescue in time to save 711 passengers.

265
00:14:36,100 --> 00:14:40,080
But 1,500 and 13 others had already perished in the icy

266
00:14:40,180 --> 00:14:41,100
water.

267
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:44,020
For the next few days, a shocked world received news

268
00:14:44,120 --> 00:14:46,320
of the tragedy via wireless.

269
00:14:46,420 --> 00:14:48,420
As each passenger was accounted for,

270
00:14:48,520 --> 00:14:51,220
the world heard about it.

271
00:14:51,320 --> 00:14:54,060
In Nantucket, Massachusetts, a young wireless operator

272
00:14:54,160 --> 00:14:56,820
for the Marconi company, David Sarnoff,

273
00:14:56,920 --> 00:15:00,520
remained at his key for 72 hours, receiving news

274
00:15:00,521 --> 00:15:03,220
of the tragedy from the rescue ship Carpathia

275
00:15:03,320 --> 00:15:05,360
as she approached New York.

276
00:15:05,460 --> 00:15:08,160
Sarnoff, a Russian immigrant with only an eighth grade

277
00:15:08,260 --> 00:15:11,000
education, had secured a position with the Marconi

278
00:15:11,100 --> 00:15:14,700
Company in New York, becoming the inventor's personal errand

279
00:15:14,800 --> 00:15:15,740
boy.

280
00:15:15,840 --> 00:15:18,940
The subsequent post as key operator at one of Marconi's land

281
00:15:19,040 --> 00:15:23,140
stations had put him in the right place at the right time.

282
00:15:23,240 --> 00:15:26,280
The Titanic tragedy had underscored the importance

283
00:15:26,380 --> 00:15:30,220
of radio to humankind in time of emergency.

284
00:15:30,221 --> 00:15:34,720
But David Sarnoff recognized radio's potential as a business.

285
00:15:34,820 --> 00:15:37,960
In 1916, now a young executive, Sarnoff

286
00:15:38,060 --> 00:15:41,860
wrote a lengthy memo to the president of American Marconi,

287
00:15:41,960 --> 00:15:43,760
wherein he outlined what he perceived

288
00:15:43,860 --> 00:15:46,560
to be the medium's future.

289
00:15:46,660 --> 00:15:48,780
I have in mind a plan of development which

290
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:51,580
would make radio a household utility.

291
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:55,020
The idea is to bring music into the home by wireless.

292
00:15:55,120 --> 00:15:57,980
The receiver can be designed in the form of a simple radio

293
00:15:57,981 --> 00:16:02,240
music box and arranged for several different wavelengths which should be

294
00:16:02,241 --> 00:16:06,920
changeable with the throwing of a single switch or pressing of a single button.

295
00:16:06,921 --> 00:16:11,780
Also, events of national importance can be simultaneously announced and received.

296
00:16:11,781 --> 00:16:18,891
Premeditated invasion. Baseball scores can be transmitted in the air.

297
00:16:18,892 --> 00:16:20,540
This proposition

298
00:16:20,541 --> 00:16:24,560
would be especially interesting to farmers and others living in outlying districts.

299
00:16:24,561 --> 00:16:26,560
Sincerely yours, David Sarnoff.

300
00:16:29,560 --> 00:16:33,360
As the specter of World War loomed on America's horizon,

301
00:16:33,460 --> 00:16:36,480
the fledgling radio industry was already engaged

302
00:16:36,580 --> 00:16:38,980
in a war of its own.

303
00:16:39,080 --> 00:16:42,080
Powerful corporations dueled in court for the patents

304
00:16:42,180 --> 00:16:44,280
that would allow them to gain a stranglehold

305
00:16:44,380 --> 00:16:46,320
on the new medium.

306
00:16:46,420 --> 00:16:49,360
No more would inventors be permitted to freely adapt

307
00:16:49,460 --> 00:16:53,280
or build upon the work of others for the good of all humanity.

308
00:16:53,281 --> 00:16:55,920
Radio was now a product that could be sold,

309
00:16:56,020 --> 00:16:59,580
and there was money to be made.

310
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:02,280
America's entry into World War I actually

311
00:17:02,380 --> 00:17:04,460
helped the radio industry.

312
00:17:04,560 --> 00:17:07,600
The government declared a moratorium on patent suits

313
00:17:07,700 --> 00:17:10,000
and resolved disputes by pooling the patents

314
00:17:10,100 --> 00:17:12,940
and allowing all companies to draw from the pool.

315
00:17:13,040 --> 00:17:15,800
This allowed the industry to concentrate on development

316
00:17:15,900 --> 00:17:18,400
and manufacturing instead of their legal squabbles.

317
00:17:18,500 --> 00:17:21,440
Soon radios were standardized and mass-produced

318
00:17:21,441 --> 00:17:24,640
to meet wartime demand.

319
00:17:24,740 --> 00:17:26,280
While serving with the army in Europe,

320
00:17:26,380 --> 00:17:29,080
Edwin Howard Armstrong continued his research

321
00:17:29,180 --> 00:17:31,620
into high frequency radio waves.

322
00:17:31,720 --> 00:17:34,660
The result of his work was a more efficient radio receiver

323
00:17:34,760 --> 00:17:36,960
he called a super heterodyne.

324
00:17:37,060 --> 00:17:39,500
The super heterodyne improved radio reception

325
00:17:39,600 --> 00:17:43,960
by narrowing the frequency range handled by amplifiers.

326
00:17:44,060 --> 00:17:47,400
This helped eliminate unwanted electrical interference

327
00:17:47,500 --> 00:17:50,260
while still boosting the radio signal.

328
00:17:50,261 --> 00:17:54,500
Armstrong's super heterodyne, though complex in circuitry,

329
00:17:54,600 --> 00:17:56,100
was simple to operate.

330
00:17:56,200 --> 00:17:59,200
With just one knob for volume and one for tuning,

331
00:17:59,300 --> 00:18:03,200
it was the first modern radio receiver.

332
00:18:03,300 --> 00:18:05,800
At the end of the war, the US government understood

333
00:18:05,900 --> 00:18:08,840
how important radio had become and moved to keep the industry

334
00:18:08,940 --> 00:18:11,920
from slipping into foreign hands.

335
00:18:12,020 --> 00:18:14,120
The Radio Corporation of America

336
00:18:14,220 --> 00:18:18,320
was formed with General Electric as the parent company.

337
00:18:18,321 --> 00:18:21,060
The patents and assets of American Marconi

338
00:18:21,160 --> 00:18:23,420
were transferred to the new company.

339
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:26,060
In return, British Marconi could continue

340
00:18:26,160 --> 00:18:28,960
to buy American parts for the equipment it manufactured

341
00:18:29,060 --> 00:18:31,560
and sold to the rest of the world.

342
00:18:31,660 --> 00:18:33,840
Most of American Marconi's employees,

343
00:18:33,940 --> 00:18:37,080
including David Sarnoff, moved to RCA.

344
00:18:37,180 --> 00:18:41,040
David Sarnoff still harbored his dream of selling radio music

345
00:18:41,140 --> 00:18:43,980
boxes for use in private homes.

346
00:18:44,080 --> 00:18:48,320
But another company, Westinghouse, beat him to his goal.

347
00:18:48,321 --> 00:18:50,880
Westinghouse had purchased the exclusive rights

348
00:18:50,881 --> 00:18:53,800
to Armstrong's super heterodyne receiver.

349
00:18:53,801 --> 00:18:55,660
And now they determined their best entry

350
00:18:55,661 --> 00:18:59,040
would be to broadcast programming from radio towers,

351
00:18:59,041 --> 00:19:01,900
and then sell the receivers to the public.

352
00:19:01,901 --> 00:19:05,380
Westinghouse chose Dr. Frank Conrad to build a radio station

353
00:19:05,381 --> 00:19:07,740
on the roof of its factory in Pittsburgh.

354
00:19:07,741 --> 00:19:11,800
Station KDKA went on the air November 2, 1920,

355
00:19:11,801 --> 00:19:15,120
broadcasting the presidential election returns.

356
00:19:15,121 --> 00:19:19,320
as soon as they are received by us here at KDKA.

357
00:19:19,420 --> 00:19:21,660
The broadcast was a huge success.

358
00:19:21,760 --> 00:19:24,220
Sales of radio receivers skyrocketed.

359
00:19:24,320 --> 00:19:27,400
In short order, KDKA and other stations

360
00:19:27,500 --> 00:19:34,600
began broadcasting church services and then boxing matches.

361
00:19:34,700 --> 00:19:37,440
Radio took off and never looked back.

362
00:19:41,580 --> 00:19:43,780
Up until now, communications companies

363
00:19:43,781 --> 00:19:46,180
had made their money through two-way communications

364
00:19:46,280 --> 00:19:50,360
charges like the telegraph, telephone, and now the wireless,

365
00:19:50,460 --> 00:19:53,660
or through the sale of radio equipment.

366
00:19:53,760 --> 00:19:57,860
On August 28, 1922, WEAF in New York

367
00:19:57,960 --> 00:20:01,460
broadcast an advertisement for an apartment building.

368
00:20:01,560 --> 00:20:04,360
It was the first paid radio commercial.

369
00:20:04,460 --> 00:20:06,340
Suddenly, a new world of enterprise

370
00:20:06,440 --> 00:20:07,700
appeared at radio's door.

371
00:20:07,800 --> 00:20:08,700
Present.

372
00:20:08,800 --> 00:20:10,140
Henry!

373
00:20:10,240 --> 00:20:11,100
Advertising.

374
00:20:11,200 --> 00:20:11,940
Henry Aldrich.

375
00:20:12,040 --> 00:20:13,780
Coming, mother.

376
00:20:13,781 --> 00:20:16,280
Radio broadcasting networks were formed

377
00:20:16,380 --> 00:20:20,280
when the Federal Trade Commission ruled that RCA, AT&T,

378
00:20:20,380 --> 00:20:24,020
and other companies had created a monopoly in the new industry.

379
00:20:24,120 --> 00:20:26,520
The national broadcasting company NBC

380
00:20:26,620 --> 00:20:30,800
was formed from RCA and Westinghouse in 1926.

381
00:20:30,900 --> 00:20:34,860
And the Columbia Broadcasting Service followed in 1929.

382
00:20:34,960 --> 00:20:38,580
Both networks were permitted to use AT&T's phone lines

383
00:20:38,680 --> 00:20:40,540
to connect their transmitters.

384
00:20:40,640 --> 00:20:42,980
The networks allowed people throughout the country

385
00:20:42,981 --> 00:20:45,980
to hear broadcast simultaneously.

386
00:20:46,080 --> 00:20:49,380
Overnight, radio began to affect every American life,

387
00:20:49,480 --> 00:20:52,620
as no other technological influence ever had.

388
00:20:52,720 --> 00:20:55,060
The sale of radio sets grew so fast

389
00:20:55,160 --> 00:20:57,560
that the manufacturers were hard pressed

390
00:20:57,660 --> 00:20:59,960
to keep up with demand.

391
00:21:00,060 --> 00:21:03,540
Radio made the world smaller just that fast

392
00:21:03,640 --> 00:21:05,440
because it could be transmitted so quickly,

393
00:21:05,540 --> 00:21:07,140
and you didn't have to wait for the newspapers

394
00:21:07,240 --> 00:21:09,880
to let you know what was happening.

395
00:21:09,980 --> 00:21:12,980
In January 1925, 15 years

396
00:21:12,981 --> 00:21:15,720
people heard Calvin Coolidge's inaugural speech

397
00:21:15,820 --> 00:21:17,220
on their radios.

398
00:21:17,320 --> 00:21:19,520
So many advertisers flocked to the new medium

399
00:21:19,620 --> 00:21:21,420
that some newspaper chains panicked

400
00:21:21,520 --> 00:21:24,060
and refused to carry radio listings or even mentioned

401
00:21:24,160 --> 00:21:27,100
the word radio in their pages.

402
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:30,760
In 1927, the first coast to coast program was broadcast

403
00:21:30,860 --> 00:21:31,960
on NBC.

404
00:21:32,060 --> 00:21:34,100
It was the Rose Bowl football game.

405
00:21:34,200 --> 00:21:36,640
Probably the biggest radio event of the 20s

406
00:21:36,740 --> 00:21:39,280
was Charles A. Lindbergh's historic solo flight

407
00:21:39,380 --> 00:21:40,380
across the Atlantic.

408
00:21:42,980 --> 00:21:45,720
This is WJZ, New York.

409
00:21:45,820 --> 00:21:48,580
As Saturday dawned over the vast Atlantic,

410
00:21:48,680 --> 00:21:51,380
the Lindbergh plane is unreported since passing

411
00:21:51,480 --> 00:21:53,120
Newfoundland early last night.

412
00:21:53,220 --> 00:21:55,300
Socially, it made a big difference.

413
00:21:55,400 --> 00:21:58,300
Somebody who was a farmer in Kansas

414
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:03,100
could be knowing the same things that somebody on Wall Street

415
00:22:03,200 --> 00:22:04,640
was hearing.

416
00:22:04,740 --> 00:22:07,300
Each year, the quality of radio programming improved

417
00:22:07,400 --> 00:22:09,480
and the number of shows multiplied.

418
00:22:09,580 --> 00:22:11,580
Performers and personalities heretofore

419
00:22:11,581 --> 00:22:15,180
heretofore unknown to a wide public found themselves thrust

420
00:22:15,280 --> 00:22:19,520
into the national spotlight overnight.

421
00:22:19,620 --> 00:22:24,320
Names like Milton Cross, Harold Arlen, Ed Wynn, Guy Lombardo

422
00:22:24,420 --> 00:22:28,340
and his Royal Canadians, Gertrude Berg and Will Rogers

423
00:22:28,440 --> 00:22:31,940
became familiar in households all over the country.

424
00:22:32,040 --> 00:22:34,040
Mr. Sloblato will now...

425
00:22:34,140 --> 00:22:37,240
Entertainment itself was changing to fit the new medium.

426
00:22:37,340 --> 00:22:39,940
Billy Jones and Ernie Hare were the first team

427
00:22:39,941 --> 00:22:41,740
to emerge from vaudeville to star on radio

428
00:22:41,840 --> 00:22:44,680
with their simple harmonies and satirical humor.

429
00:22:44,780 --> 00:22:46,980
They billed themselves as the happiness boys.

430
00:22:47,080 --> 00:22:48,680
Once upon a time.

431
00:22:48,780 --> 00:22:50,680
As the decade drew to a close,

432
00:22:50,780 --> 00:22:54,060
David Sarnoff at NBC and his new young rival at CBS,

433
00:22:54,160 --> 00:22:58,060
William Paley, were drawing lines on the battlefield of radio.

434
00:22:58,160 --> 00:23:02,660
At that time, CBS, when I first joined them,

435
00:23:02,760 --> 00:23:06,040
they were like a family network.

436
00:23:06,140 --> 00:23:08,640
NBC was a big power.

437
00:23:08,641 --> 00:23:13,640
And they were not going to try to compete.

438
00:23:13,740 --> 00:23:16,680
They didn't have anybody to put up against Bob Hope.

439
00:23:16,780 --> 00:23:21,280
They would have had to have Jack Benny or Fred Allen.

440
00:23:21,380 --> 00:23:23,080
But NBC had them both.

441
00:23:23,180 --> 00:23:28,800
It was not many years before William Paley raided NBC

442
00:23:28,900 --> 00:23:31,900
and got Jack Benny away from them.

443
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:34,940
And then the picture changed, you know.

444
00:23:35,040 --> 00:23:37,660
In 1929, the stock market crashed

445
00:23:37,661 --> 00:23:40,600
pitched the nation headlong into a Great Depression.

446
00:23:40,700 --> 00:23:42,800
There would be dark days ahead for America,

447
00:23:42,900 --> 00:23:45,360
and she would turn to her new best friend, the radio,

448
00:23:45,460 --> 00:23:46,400
to see her through.

449
00:23:51,300 --> 00:23:56,780
Radio, out of thin air, now continues on Modern Marvels.

450
00:23:56,880 --> 00:23:58,780
Radio couldn't lose.

451
00:23:58,880 --> 00:24:01,320
The stock market crash and advent of the Depression

452
00:24:01,420 --> 00:24:04,420
did nothing to cool America's ardor for the magic box

453
00:24:04,520 --> 00:24:06,220
with the dials on it.

454
00:24:06,221 --> 00:24:09,460
Theaters closed down, nightclubs languished.

455
00:24:09,560 --> 00:24:11,820
Theatrical stock companies disappeared,

456
00:24:11,920 --> 00:24:14,000
but radio prospered.

457
00:24:14,100 --> 00:24:16,360
Radio was very important in the Depression

458
00:24:16,460 --> 00:24:19,340
because it was cheap entertainment.

459
00:24:19,440 --> 00:24:22,600
If you didn't have the buck to go to the movie,

460
00:24:22,700 --> 00:24:24,980
you stayed at home and you heard some good stuff.

461
00:24:25,080 --> 00:24:27,880
And Ed Wynn, the fire chief.

462
00:24:27,980 --> 00:24:33,940
Between 1930 and 1933, 4 million radios

463
00:24:33,941 --> 00:24:35,880
were sold in America.

464
00:24:35,980 --> 00:24:38,040
Now, why radio?

465
00:24:38,140 --> 00:24:41,280
Because what you bought was free entertainment,

466
00:24:41,380 --> 00:24:43,580
paid for by the commercials.

467
00:24:43,680 --> 00:24:49,720
Who knows what evil lurks in the hearts of men?

468
00:24:49,820 --> 00:24:53,220
The shadow knows.

469
00:24:53,320 --> 00:24:56,660
By 1930, network programming had assumed the shape

470
00:24:56,760 --> 00:24:59,200
it would retain for the next 20 years.

471
00:24:59,300 --> 00:25:02,340
The broadcast day was divided into quarter, half,

472
00:25:02,341 --> 00:25:05,100
quarter, half, and full hour segments.

473
00:25:05,200 --> 00:25:09,780
Programs and commercials were timed to the second.

474
00:25:09,880 --> 00:25:13,380
Radio's appetite for material was voracious.

475
00:25:13,480 --> 00:25:16,420
All the hours of the day and night needed to be filled.

476
00:25:16,520 --> 00:25:19,060
Syndication of scripts and ready-made programs

477
00:25:19,160 --> 00:25:22,500
to stations all over the country became big business

478
00:25:22,600 --> 00:25:25,000
and standardized the broadcast industry.

479
00:25:25,100 --> 00:25:27,400
Stations began to sound alike.

480
00:25:27,500 --> 00:25:30,340
Radio settled into a comfortable and perhaps

481
00:25:30,341 --> 00:25:32,340
of the center of the culture.

482
00:25:32,440 --> 00:25:34,680
The networks were aware of the hold they had

483
00:25:34,780 --> 00:25:37,080
on the country's attention and were determined not

484
00:25:37,180 --> 00:25:38,780
to choke the golden goose.

485
00:25:38,880 --> 00:25:41,580
Radio produced stars no less in stature

486
00:25:41,680 --> 00:25:43,580
than ever pranced upon a Broadway stage

487
00:25:43,680 --> 00:25:45,880
or kissed on a Hollywood screen.

488
00:25:45,980 --> 00:25:48,920
America embraced these performers and personalities

489
00:25:49,020 --> 00:25:51,600
as if they were family, and in a way they were.

490
00:25:51,700 --> 00:25:54,200
You had to go to a theater to see movie stars,

491
00:25:54,300 --> 00:25:57,740
but a radio performer came right into your living room.

492
00:25:57,741 --> 00:26:00,380
Just about seven nights a week, I think,

493
00:26:00,480 --> 00:26:04,040
you could turn on your radio after 6.30 or 7 in the evening,

494
00:26:04,140 --> 00:26:08,280
and until about 11, be assured of really good entertainment.

495
00:26:08,380 --> 00:26:10,880
We these breakfast of champions

496
00:26:10,980 --> 00:26:14,220
bring you the thrilling adventures of Jack Armstrong,

497
00:26:14,320 --> 00:26:16,620
the all-American boy.

498
00:26:19,160 --> 00:26:21,100
From Hollywood, the Jimmy Durante Show.

499
00:26:24,200 --> 00:26:26,360
The makers of Instant Chase and Sanborn Coffee

500
00:26:26,361 --> 00:26:29,360
The Unborn Coffee presents the Charlie McCarthy Show.

501
00:26:29,460 --> 00:26:31,060
Edgar Berg and Charlie McCarthy.

502
00:26:31,160 --> 00:26:32,860
How could a ventriloquist work on a radio?

503
00:26:32,960 --> 00:26:33,760
Charlie was special.

504
00:26:33,860 --> 00:26:34,860
He was hysterical.

505
00:26:34,960 --> 00:26:37,600
Well, we want evil building and we want more money.

506
00:26:39,800 --> 00:26:43,520
Every show was an experience.

507
00:26:43,620 --> 00:26:46,320
Every show, you look forward going to work.

508
00:26:46,420 --> 00:26:49,020
The sound effects men were there, the unsung heroes

509
00:26:49,120 --> 00:26:50,620
of early day radio.

510
00:26:50,720 --> 00:26:54,920
Good sound effects men could make or break it.

511
00:26:54,921 --> 00:26:57,920
I gotta straighten out that closet one of these days.

512
00:26:59,920 --> 00:27:07,920
They knew that they wanted to have something aside from a fiery horse with the speed of light, a cloud of dust, and a hearty...

513
00:27:07,921 --> 00:27:10,920
And Fran Stryker, who wrote the show, had written in,

514
00:27:10,921 --> 00:27:12,920
And a hearty laugh.

515
00:27:12,921 --> 00:27:15,920
And this is three minutes before they're going on the air live.

516
00:27:15,921 --> 00:27:17,920
No, we can't have a laugh. What do we use?

517
00:27:17,921 --> 00:27:21,920
Can't he say something like cowboy something?

518
00:27:21,921 --> 00:27:23,920
Like yip, yip, yip, yip?

519
00:27:23,921 --> 00:27:25,020
No, no, that doesn't.

520
00:27:25,021 --> 00:27:26,620
How about talking to his horse?

521
00:27:26,621 --> 00:27:27,321
Yeah.

522
00:27:27,322 --> 00:27:30,820
How about hi-oh, hi-oh, hi-oh silver away?

523
00:27:30,821 --> 00:27:31,521
That's it.

524
00:27:31,522 --> 00:27:34,420
So they ran into the studio and that's the way it happened.

525
00:27:35,820 --> 00:27:39,420
Hi-oh silver away!

526
