597
00:31:05,740 --> 00:31:09,480
the bodies that fell maybe 150 or 200 feet.

598
00:31:09,580 --> 00:31:12,480
Arguably, the era's single most adept figure

599
00:31:12,481 --> 00:31:15,120
in exploiting the power and influence of radio

600
00:31:15,220 --> 00:31:17,780
was President Franklin Delano Roosevelt.

601
00:31:17,880 --> 00:31:20,960
I am happy to speak to you from my home

602
00:31:21,060 --> 00:31:23,760
on a Sabbath day that has been observed

603
00:31:23,860 --> 00:31:28,860
in so many of your home communities as Brotherhood Day.

604
00:31:28,960 --> 00:31:32,660
He talked to everybody without either talking down or up.

605
00:31:32,760 --> 00:31:36,700
And I think that he kept the notion of the nation

606
00:31:36,800 --> 00:31:38,240
tightly bound.

607
00:31:38,340 --> 00:31:42,480
Let me assert my firm belief that the only

608
00:31:42,481 --> 00:31:48,720
The only thing we have to fear is fear itself.

609
00:31:48,820 --> 00:31:52,560
Nameless, unreasoning, unjustified terror,

610
00:31:52,660 --> 00:31:58,060
which paralyzes needed efforts to convert retreat into advance.

611
00:31:58,061 --> 00:32:12,061
America's dreams and hopes of peace died with 3,000 officers and men in the Holocaust at Pearl Harbor.

612
00:32:17,980 --> 00:32:25,580
Radio's role in World War II was a very dramatic one in that for the first time we were getting reports

613
00:32:25,581 --> 00:32:29,780
from correspondence abroad, live in the capitals.

614
00:32:29,880 --> 00:32:33,960
As the seeds of war began to grow and the menace increased,

615
00:32:34,060 --> 00:32:36,460
it was a very tense time.

616
00:32:36,560 --> 00:32:40,860
And we depended upon radio to bring us the latest.

617
00:32:40,960 --> 00:32:44,800
World War II really added a new dimension to radio.

618
00:32:44,900 --> 00:32:47,060
And that was news.

619
00:32:47,160 --> 00:32:51,040
The war also demonstrated the use of radio as a weapon.

620
00:32:51,140 --> 00:32:53,000
It was called propaganda.

621
00:32:53,001 --> 00:32:58,040
Her name was Eva Tokuri Dakino, but they called her Tokyo Rose.

622
00:32:58,140 --> 00:33:01,940
During World War II, her radio broadcasts to American GIs

623
00:33:02,040 --> 00:33:04,840
became both famous and infamous.

624
00:33:04,940 --> 00:33:08,620
Radio kept the nation united and focused on the war effort.

625
00:33:08,720 --> 00:33:10,320
Entertainers took to the airwaves

626
00:33:10,420 --> 00:33:12,960
to rally support for the boys.

627
00:33:13,060 --> 00:33:16,960
As far back as 1933, Edwin Howard Armstrong

628
00:33:17,060 --> 00:33:19,560
had developed frequency modulation, or FM,

629
00:33:19,660 --> 00:33:20,960
as the answer to static.

630
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:25,040
When America entered World War II,

631
00:33:25,140 --> 00:33:27,140
Armstrong petitioned the army and navy

632
00:33:27,240 --> 00:33:30,740
to adopt FM as the format for two-way communication

633
00:33:30,840 --> 00:33:32,140
systems.

634
00:33:32,240 --> 00:33:36,180
Staticless FM communication gave the Allies an advantage

635
00:33:36,280 --> 00:33:39,580
over the Germans with their AM walkie talkies.

636
00:33:39,680 --> 00:33:43,060
FM helped win the war.

637
00:33:43,160 --> 00:33:46,420
Back home, the golden age of radio was going strong.

638
00:33:46,520 --> 00:33:50,760
Popular entertainers of the 40s included Frank Sinatra, Glenn

639
00:33:50,761 --> 00:33:55,200
Glenn Miller, Benny Goodman, Rosemary Clooney, Arthur

640
00:33:55,300 --> 00:33:58,000
Godfrey, Abbott and Costello, Burns and Allen,

641
00:33:58,100 --> 00:34:01,540
and Basil Rathbone as Sherlock Holmes.

642
00:34:01,640 --> 00:34:04,340
For the long-haired crowd, there was Arturo Toscanini

643
00:34:04,440 --> 00:34:06,440
with the NBC Symphony Orchestra.

644
00:34:06,540 --> 00:34:09,020
The golden age of radio was the shortest golden age

645
00:34:09,120 --> 00:34:10,440
in history.

646
00:34:10,540 --> 00:34:12,780
And those who were working inside it

647
00:34:12,880 --> 00:34:14,760
were not aware that it was the golden age.

648
00:34:14,860 --> 00:34:16,220
They thought it would go on forever.

649
00:34:16,320 --> 00:34:18,980
But forever was just around the corner.

650
00:34:18,981 --> 00:34:23,460
The end of World War II brought a new era of growth and prosperity to America,

651
00:34:23,461 --> 00:34:27,320
and radio occupied a position at the very center of the culture.

652
00:34:27,820 --> 00:34:33,080
She had no way of knowing it, but the wolf was at the door, and its name was television.

653
00:34:37,840 --> 00:34:41,820
There is a radio in over 70% of American bedrooms.

654
00:34:42,240 --> 00:34:44,120
Most of them are clock radios.

655
00:34:44,720 --> 00:34:46,960
Radio Out of Thin Air will continue in a moment.

656
00:34:48,980 --> 00:34:55,980
Radio out of thin air now continues on Modern Marvels.

657
00:34:55,981 --> 00:34:58,980
Television knocked radio for a loop.

658
00:34:58,981 --> 00:35:03,980
Overnight, TV did to radio what the talkies had done to silent pictures.

659
00:35:03,981 --> 00:35:08,980
Television had been put on hold during the war, but now came on strong as millions of young Americans,

660
00:35:08,981 --> 00:35:16,980
children of the Depression and weary of the war years, settled down and started living the American dream.

661
00:35:16,981 --> 00:35:20,680
GI's came home with a lot of money in their pockets,

662
00:35:20,780 --> 00:35:26,420
and they found that they could buy a new medium for a new era,

663
00:35:26,520 --> 00:35:28,360
and that was television.

664
00:35:28,460 --> 00:35:31,320
Many of the performers who had made their names on radio

665
00:35:31,420 --> 00:35:33,460
found new homes on TV.

666
00:35:33,560 --> 00:35:35,000
The public, intrigued with the idea

667
00:35:35,100 --> 00:35:37,760
of seeing their favorite personalities as well as hearing

668
00:35:37,860 --> 00:35:40,040
them, followed along eagerly.

669
00:35:40,140 --> 00:35:41,780
It didn't take long for television

670
00:35:41,880 --> 00:35:45,240
to usurp the evening hours from radio.

671
00:35:45,241 --> 00:35:48,480
Radio appealed to the innocence in me.

672
00:35:48,580 --> 00:35:49,580
Television took it away.

673
00:35:49,680 --> 00:35:51,180
There's no innocence on television.

674
00:35:51,280 --> 00:35:53,180
It's right there in front of you.

675
00:35:53,280 --> 00:35:54,880
Rod Serling told me once, he said,

676
00:35:54,980 --> 00:35:58,380
you know, when I wrote for radio, I could write,

677
00:35:58,480 --> 00:36:03,900
there's a dark, foreboding castle at the top of a hill.

678
00:36:04,000 --> 00:36:06,360
And if you could say that with a deep voice

679
00:36:06,460 --> 00:36:10,140
and the right organ music, whew.

680
00:36:10,240 --> 00:36:12,240
If I write that same thing for television,

681
00:36:12,340 --> 00:36:13,800
two technicians come in and say, well,

682
00:36:13,801 --> 00:36:15,260
Well, what kind of castle do you want, Rod?

683
00:36:15,261 --> 00:36:16,100
You want peaks?

684
00:36:16,101 --> 00:36:16,900
You want little lights on?

685
00:36:16,901 --> 00:36:18,000
Where do you want the lighting to go?

686
00:36:18,001 --> 00:36:19,900
And I'm showing you the castle.

687
00:36:19,901 --> 00:36:21,100
Where is radio?

688
00:36:21,101 --> 00:36:22,940
Let me invent my own castle.

689
00:36:22,941 --> 00:36:24,400
Innocence.

690
00:36:24,401 --> 00:36:27,040
The early 50s saw the networks.

691
00:36:27,041 --> 00:36:30,620
There were three by now rushing to establish superiority

692
00:36:30,621 --> 00:36:31,980
in the new media.

693
00:36:31,981 --> 00:36:36,860
For a time, radio floundered, seeking to reinvent itself.

694
00:36:36,861 --> 00:36:39,800
The public had demonstrated its preference for television

695
00:36:39,801 --> 00:36:43,800
in the evenings, but that left mornings and afternoons open.

696
00:36:43,801 --> 00:36:48,100
Car radios had become every bit as popular as the cars themselves,

697
00:36:48,400 --> 00:36:51,840
and TV could not very well invade that territory.

698
00:36:52,380 --> 00:36:56,860
At last, radio found a new direction by playing to its strengths.

699
00:36:57,840 --> 00:37:00,140
TV, I think, probably did radio a favor,

700
00:37:00,500 --> 00:37:04,240
because look at radio today and look at radio in the Golden Age.

701
00:37:04,520 --> 00:37:07,360
In the Golden Age, in the 30s and the 40s, it thrived,

702
00:37:07,620 --> 00:37:09,540
and there were shows on radio.

703
00:37:10,200 --> 00:37:12,900
All of a sudden, those shows moved to television.

704
00:37:12,901 --> 00:37:16,400
And when that happened, radio didn't know what to fall back on.

705
00:37:16,500 --> 00:37:21,140
And then along came a guy named Elvis Presley and rock and roll.

706
00:37:21,240 --> 00:37:23,840
And all of a sudden, radio found itself

707
00:37:23,940 --> 00:37:26,340
and knew exactly the direction it should take.

708
00:37:26,440 --> 00:37:30,360
It was radio became local, radio became music,

709
00:37:30,460 --> 00:37:33,160
and radio thrived.

710
00:37:33,260 --> 00:37:35,060
Rock and roll saved radio.

711
00:37:35,160 --> 00:37:38,300
You're talking about a wonderful part of our culture

712
00:37:38,400 --> 00:37:41,800
that now is primarily in the automobile, the car.

713
00:37:41,801 --> 00:37:43,900
The car is the savior of radio.

714
00:37:44,000 --> 00:37:46,600
The big advertisers moved to television,

715
00:37:46,700 --> 00:37:49,300
and the program budgets went with them.

716
00:37:49,400 --> 00:37:52,540
No longer were shows prepackaged for radio stations.

717
00:37:52,640 --> 00:37:54,340
The stations found themselves back

718
00:37:54,440 --> 00:37:56,840
where they had started in the days before syndication

719
00:37:56,940 --> 00:37:59,460
with a lot of open time to fill.

720
00:37:59,560 --> 00:38:02,160
Gone were the soap operas, the spooky dramas,

721
00:38:02,260 --> 00:38:04,060
adventures, and familiar comedies.

722
00:38:04,160 --> 00:38:05,860
Gone to TV.

723
00:38:05,960 --> 00:38:09,200
All that was left behind was a turntable and a microphone.

724
00:38:09,300 --> 00:38:11,560
And now let me tell you about McDonough.

725
00:38:11,561 --> 00:38:14,060
McTavish's skinless weanings.

726
00:38:14,061 --> 00:38:15,800
Mm-mm.

727
00:38:15,801 --> 00:38:18,200
People still wanted music and news and weather

728
00:38:18,201 --> 00:38:19,760
and local information.

729
00:38:19,761 --> 00:38:21,940
So that's what radio became.

730
00:38:21,941 --> 00:38:25,080
Popular music became the top 40, and the station announcer

731
00:38:25,081 --> 00:38:28,480
became a disc jockey.

732
00:38:28,481 --> 00:38:30,780
Radio set about becoming what it might have been

733
00:38:30,781 --> 00:38:33,380
had the networks not jumped right in at the outset,

734
00:38:33,381 --> 00:38:36,780
as David Sarnoff had predicted in his memo more than 30 years

735
00:38:36,781 --> 00:38:41,060
before, radio would play music and broadcast ball games.

736
00:38:41,061 --> 00:38:43,520
The Slashen Drive, after what let's send it to you.

737
00:38:43,620 --> 00:38:47,700
In October 1954, the first portable transistor radios

738
00:38:47,800 --> 00:38:49,540
began showing up in stores.

739
00:38:49,640 --> 00:38:52,340
The transistor had been developed at the end of the war

740
00:38:52,440 --> 00:38:55,000
by engineers at Bell Laboratories.

741
00:38:55,100 --> 00:38:57,440
It was far smaller and more inexpensive

742
00:38:57,540 --> 00:38:59,180
to make than the vacuum tube.

743
00:38:59,280 --> 00:39:02,420
The transistor gave radio another well-needed boost.

744
00:39:02,520 --> 00:39:05,380
Now it could go anywhere, to the beach or the picnic

745
00:39:05,480 --> 00:39:06,820
or the ball game.

746
00:39:06,920 --> 00:39:10,120
By the late 50s, for each television set owned in the US,

747
00:39:10,121 --> 00:39:11,620
there were three radios.

748
00:39:14,120 --> 00:39:17,420
FM's moment finally arrived in the mid-'50s when stereo

749
00:39:17,520 --> 00:39:19,500
recordings and stereo record players

750
00:39:19,600 --> 00:39:21,100
debuted on the market.

751
00:39:21,200 --> 00:39:25,600
Stereo provided two channels of separated sound, which greatly

752
00:39:25,700 --> 00:39:28,140
enhanced the listening experience.

753
00:39:28,240 --> 00:39:30,400
FM, with its wider bandwidth, was

754
00:39:30,500 --> 00:39:33,180
capable of transmitting and receiving these stereo

755
00:39:33,280 --> 00:39:36,920
signals with very high and low frequencies.

756
00:39:37,020 --> 00:39:38,980
As listener interest in FM increased,

757
00:39:38,981 --> 00:39:41,480
the number of stations grew to meet the demand to the point

758
00:39:41,580 --> 00:39:46,320
where FM stations outnumbered those broadcasting in AM.

759
00:39:46,420 --> 00:39:48,860
As stereo sound advanced, listeners

760
00:39:48,960 --> 00:39:51,420
became more sophisticated and discriminating

761
00:39:51,520 --> 00:39:53,460
in their listening tastes.

762
00:39:53,560 --> 00:39:56,760
While AM clung to the increasingly younger top 40

763
00:39:56,860 --> 00:39:59,600
listener, the more educated, affluent listeners

764
00:39:59,700 --> 00:40:03,200
switched to FM, and the advertisers followed them.

765
00:40:03,300 --> 00:40:06,800
FM with more stations divided itself into formats

766
00:40:06,801 --> 00:40:10,340
to more specifically target a desired group,

767
00:40:10,440 --> 00:40:13,900
thereby offering clearer alternatives for advertisers.

768
00:40:14,000 --> 00:40:16,240
A station that broadcast romantic songs

769
00:40:16,340 --> 00:40:19,040
might appeal to a younger female audience,

770
00:40:19,140 --> 00:40:21,540
while a harder edge album rock formula

771
00:40:21,640 --> 00:40:24,360
would be more attractive to males.

772
00:40:24,460 --> 00:40:27,760
AM, in danger of losing the entire adult market to FM,

773
00:40:27,860 --> 00:40:34,560
found salvation and new life in a format called simply talk.

774
00:40:34,561 --> 00:40:38,600
The average American household has 5.7 radios.

