00:41:08,720 --> 00:41:11,420
They know by putting people on the air, just listeners,

784
00:41:11,520 --> 00:41:13,720
on the telephone, giving their opinion

785
00:41:13,820 --> 00:41:15,960
that they will capture the imagination of people just

786
00:41:16,060 --> 00:41:17,500
listening vicariously.

787
00:41:17,600 --> 00:41:19,640
As more listening alternatives appeared

788
00:41:19,740 --> 00:41:21,860
on the radio dial, the audience became more

789
00:41:21,960 --> 00:41:22,940
and more fragmented.

790
00:41:25,940 --> 00:41:28,140
More choices for advertisers meant

791
00:41:28,240 --> 00:41:29,800
that stations had to compete harder

792
00:41:29,801 --> 00:41:32,300
for their share of the advertising dollars.

793
00:41:32,400 --> 00:41:35,040
Rating services like Arbitron and Birch

794
00:41:35,140 --> 00:41:38,000
began to pull the listening public to find out who was

795
00:41:38,100 --> 00:41:40,740
listening and to what station.

796
00:41:40,840 --> 00:41:43,440
The broadcast industry broke its audience down

797
00:41:43,540 --> 00:41:47,460
into categories according to age and sex and income.

798
00:41:47,560 --> 00:41:50,660
The analysis was called demographics.

799
00:41:50,760 --> 00:41:54,360
Today, radio is really ruled by this, Arbitron.

800
00:41:54,460 --> 00:41:57,300
Arbitron is the rating service that

801
00:41:57,301 --> 00:42:00,680
that is used to measure ratings for radio.

802
00:42:00,681 --> 00:42:03,400
The ratings will indicate who's number one, number two,

803
00:42:03,401 --> 00:42:07,220
number 10, number 15 in different age groups.

804
00:42:07,221 --> 00:42:09,960
And the advertising community uses that tool

805
00:42:09,961 --> 00:42:13,380
to decide how much advertising or whether to place advertising

806
00:42:13,381 --> 00:42:15,560
on radio stations.

807
00:42:15,561 --> 00:42:19,540
There's very few stations left where the disc jockeys

808
00:42:19,541 --> 00:42:21,060
pick their own music.

809
00:42:21,061 --> 00:42:26,360
And I'm reluctant to burst that myth, but that's the case.

810
00:42:26,361 --> 00:42:29,560
The music is pre-programmed into a computer,

811
00:42:29,561 --> 00:42:32,160
and the computer is told by a music director

812
00:42:32,161 --> 00:42:34,560
how to rotate the songs.

813
00:42:34,561 --> 00:42:38,460
What is happening is people want immediate gratification.

814
00:42:38,461 --> 00:42:41,960
They don't want to have to wait for one type of music form

815
00:42:41,961 --> 00:42:43,760
to end before they can get another,

816
00:42:43,761 --> 00:42:46,760
when they know they can press button number two on the radio

817
00:42:46,761 --> 00:42:49,860
and get that exact music form.

818
00:42:49,861 --> 00:42:54,300
I think the rating system was perhaps the most crippling

819
00:42:54,301 --> 00:42:59,440
influence on not only radio, but television.

820
00:42:59,441 --> 00:43:02,180
I think there has been altogether a dumbing down,

821
00:43:02,181 --> 00:43:04,040
and that's a terrible thing to happen.

822
00:43:04,041 --> 00:43:07,040
After 8 o'clock, we're here, we're queer, get used to it.

823
00:43:07,041 --> 00:43:09,420
It's strange, isn't it, that in a lifetime,

824
00:43:09,421 --> 00:43:12,180
you could see the birth and the demise of an industry

825
00:43:12,181 --> 00:43:13,180
as we knew it.

826
00:43:13,181 --> 00:43:15,160
Of course, radio is stronger today than it ever was

827
00:43:15,161 --> 00:43:17,700
so far as commercials and whatnot are concerned.

828
00:43:17,701 --> 00:43:19,620
In the future, you're going to have an all-blue station,

829
00:43:19,621 --> 00:43:24,200
an all-reggae station, an all-hip-hop station.

830
00:43:24,201 --> 00:43:28,440
And that's, it's just a lot, it's reflective of the sophistication

831
00:43:28,441 --> 00:43:31,340
of the rating service.

832
00:43:31,341 --> 00:43:35,980
In 1962, the satellite Telstar was launched into orbit,

833
00:43:35,981 --> 00:43:39,280
and the communications networks were boosted into space.

834
00:43:42,160 --> 00:43:45,060
Soon, dozens of satellites were orbiting the Earth,

835
00:43:45,061 --> 00:43:50,100
relaying and amplifying signals from one station to another.

836
00:43:50,101 --> 00:43:53,640
Phone conversations, radio and television programming,

837
00:43:53,641 --> 00:43:57,980
data, all signals riding on radio waves.

838
00:43:58,080 --> 00:44:01,580
Today, life without radio would be unimaginable.

839
00:44:01,680 --> 00:44:04,520
We use it for information and entertainment.

840
00:44:04,620 --> 00:44:08,020
Radio waves cook our food, open our garage doors,

841
00:44:08,120 --> 00:44:10,360
and allow us to communicate down the block

842
00:44:10,460 --> 00:44:11,860
or far into deep space.

843
00:44:14,660 --> 00:44:17,360
Remarkable that Marconi registered his patent for

844
00:44:17,460 --> 00:44:20,840
wireless barely 100 years ago.

845
00:44:20,940 --> 00:44:23,140
Perhaps the reason we take radio for granted

846
00:44:23,141 --> 00:44:26,180
is that we understand instinctively that it has always

847
00:44:26,280 --> 00:44:29,440
been here in the air around us.

848
00:44:29,540 --> 00:44:33,280
Radio is not so much invented as plucked from the air.

849
00:44:33,380 --> 00:44:35,660
It is hard to consider how far radio has come

850
00:44:35,760 --> 00:44:40,220
in just the 20th century without wondering what marvels

851
00:44:40,320 --> 00:44:44,860
await us in the next, and realizing just how fast we

852
00:44:44,960 --> 00:44:47,400
are rushing to meet the future.

853
00:44:47,500 --> 00:44:49,400
But the answer to these human problems,

854
00:44:49,500 --> 00:44:53,140
listen in tomorrow, same time, same station.

855
00:44:53,141 --> 00:44:57,320
Time marches on.

